Whenever an API is created, modified, or removed, the corresponding Postman collection and documentation must be updated immediately. The documentation should be detailed enough that a frontend developer can integrate the API without reading the backend code.

---

## General Rules

* Every API must have exactly one Postman request.
* Update the existing request instead of creating duplicates.
* Every API change must be reflected in Postman before considering the task complete.
* Keep request and response examples synchronized with the implementation.
* Use meaningful request names and organize requests inside the correct folders.

---

# Every API Documentation Must Include

## 1. API Name

Use a clear, descriptive name.

Example:

```
Create Student
Promote Student
Get Class Sections
Update Subject Mapping
```

---

## 2. HTTP Method

Specify the method.

```
GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE
```

---

## 3. Endpoint

Example

```
POST /api/student/add
GET /api/class-section/list
```

---

## 4. Description

Explain:

* What the API does
* When it should be used
* Any important business rules
* Any dependencies

Example

```
Creates a new student and assigns the student to the selected class section.
SemesterId is no longer required.
```

---

## 5. Authentication

Clearly specify.

Example

```
Bearer Token Required
```

or

```
Public API
```

---

## 6. Headers

Document every required header.

Example

```
Authorization
Content-Type
Accept
CampusId
InstituteId
```

---

## 7. Path Parameters

Document every parameter.

Example

| Name      | Type    | Required | Description |
| --------- | ------- | -------- | ----------- |
| studentId | Integer | Yes      | Student ID  |

---

## 8. Query Parameters

Example

| Name   | Type   | Required | Default | Description |
| ------ | ------ | -------- | ------- | ----------- |
| page   | Number | No       | 1       | Page Number |
| search | String | No       | ""      | Search text |

---

## 9. Request Body

Provide a complete JSON example.

Include:

* Required fields
* Optional fields
* Nullable fields
* Default values (if applicable)

Example

```json
{
  "courseId": 1,
  "classSectionId": 5,
  "term": 2,
  "termType": "SEMESTER",
  "semesterId": null,
  "remarks": "",
  "transportId": null
}
```

---

## 10. Field Documentation

For every request field specify:

* Name
* Type
* Required
* Nullable
* Default Value
* Description

Example

| Field    | Type    | Required | Nullable | Description     |
| -------- | ------- | -------- | -------- | --------------- |
| courseId | Integer | Yes      | No       | Course ID       |
| remarks  | String  | No       | Yes      | Student remarks |

---

## 11. Enum Documentation

Whenever a field accepts limited values, document all possible options.

Example

### termType

```
SEMESTER
YEARLY
QUARTERLY
MONTHLY
```

Example

### attendanceStatus

```
MARKED
PENDING
ABSENT
PRESENT
LEAVE
HOLIDAY
```

Never document enums as free text. Always list every supported value.

---

## 12. Conditional Fields

Document fields that depend on other fields.

Example

```
specializationId

Required only when course has specialization enabled.
```

Example

```
transportId

Required only when transport is enabled.
```

---

## 13. Removed Fields

Whenever a field is deprecated or removed, explicitly document it.

Example

```
semesterId

Removed.

No longer required after the Term implementation.
```

---

## 14. Response Example

Provide a real response.

Success

```json
{
    "success": true,
    "message": "Student created successfully.",
    "data": {}
}
```

Validation Error

```json
{
    "success": false,
    "message": "Course not found."
}
```

---

## 15. Error Codes

Document every expected error.

Example

| Status | Reason                |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| 400    | Validation Error      |
| 401    | Unauthorized          |
| 403    | Forbidden             |
| 404    | Resource Not Found    |
| 409    | Duplicate Record      |
| 500    | Internal Server Error |

---

## 16. Business Rules

Document important logic.

Example

* SemesterId is no longer used.
* Promotion is based on Term.
* Academic Year changes automatically after completing the last term.
* Subject Mapping ignores deleted teachers.

---

## 17. Sample Flow

If the API is part of a workflow, document the sequence.

Example

```
Create Course
↓

Create Class Section
↓

Add Student
↓

Promote Student
↓

Generate Attendance
```

---

## 18. Changelog

Whenever an API changes, add a note.

Example

```
v2

• Removed semesterId.
• Added term.
• Added termType.
• Updated promotion logic.
```

---

# Documentation Quality Checklist

Before marking an API as complete, verify that:

* Endpoint is correct.
* HTTP method is correct.
* Authentication is documented.
* Headers are documented.
* Path parameters are documented.
* Query parameters are documented.
* Request body is complete.
* Required fields are identified.
* Optional fields are identified.
* Nullable fields are identified.
* Default values are documented.
* Every enum lists all supported values.
* Conditional fields are documented.
* Removed fields are noted.
* Success response is included.
* Error responses are included.
* Business rules are documented.
* Sample workflow is included where applicable.
* Changelog reflects the latest updates.
* Examples match the current backend implementation exactly.

Frontend developers should be able to integrate any API using the Postman documentation alone, without reading backend code.
